<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cynegils</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 642</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cynegils was king of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> from about 611 to about 642. In 635 he was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birinus">Birinus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames">Dorchester-on-Thames</a>, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a> as his godfather, making him the first Christian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. Birinus was given Dorchester as the seat of the first West Saxon bishopric, from which the conversion of the kingdom proceeded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cynegils.jpg" alt="Cynegils" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Speed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kengils_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Cynegils is traditionally considered king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, although the kingdoms of the Heptarchy had not yet formed in his lifetime. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> places his kingdom on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">River Thames</a>, extending into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames">Dorchester-on-Thames</a> as one of its major royal sites. This region, probably linked to the tribal grouping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> calls the Gewisse, lay on the frontier between the later kingdoms of Wessex and Mercia. A Celtic etymology, grey dog, has been suggested for his name, as for those of several other members of the Wessex ruling house.</p><p>He seems to have become king on the death of King Ceolwulf, around 611. West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> sources give his ancestry in several incompatible ways, as a son of Ceolwulf, of Ceol, of Ceola, of Cuthwine or of Cuthwulf. The Chronicle and the king lists show a single king, but the kingship was probably shared between two or more.</p><p>The Chronicle records that in 614 Cynegils and Cwichelm fought at Beandun and killed two thousand and forty-six of the Welsh. In 628 they fought King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a>. The Chronicle reports no victory, so Penda was probably the winner. By then the two appear to have been subject to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a>, paying a large tribute if Nick Higham's view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tribal-hidage">Tribal Hidage</a> is correct.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the 630s Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birinus">Birinus</a> established himself at Dorchester. Cynegils and Cwichelm are both said to have been baptised there, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a> as godfather, and Oswald married a daughter of Cynegils, called Kyneburga by Reginald of Durham. The mission seems to have had little lasting effect, since Cynegils's son Cenwalh was not baptised until 646, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> had driven him from his kingdom.</p><p>The date of Cynegils's death is not certainly known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says only that Cenwalh followed him. Different <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the Chronicle date Cenwalh's accession to 641 or 643, and many texts follow Dumville in taking both as errors for 642, which fits the stated reign of 31 years. Besides Cenwalh and the daughter, Cynegils is said to have had a son Cwichelm, who may be the king named beside him. The claim that King Centwine was also his son was probably a later invention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cynegils” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity arrived when Bishop Birinus baptised King <b>Cynegils</b> in 635, with Oswald of Northumbria as godfather, and established a see at Dorchester-on-Thames; the see later moved to Winchester, and a second bishopric was created at Sherborne in 705 under the scholar Aldhelm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
